@gregg-miskelly @asundheim It's probably worth calling out that this `catch (NavigationException navigationException)` can end up throwing another exception in some rare cases that would be nice to warn the user about.

https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/b5a97c44d34062a3b8eba877c71732343c1f51a9/src/Components/Endpoints/src/Rendering/EndpointHtmlRenderer.Prerendering.cs#L191-L201

Sadly, this exception is thrown without any user code on the stack. We could try breaking for the original `NavigationException` in this particular case, which was presumably thrown by user code. But that by itself doesn't inform the user why this `NavigationException` was problematic unlike others (which is because the response has already started and streaming rendering is not enabled). Maybe that's still better than not breaking at all in this case though.

@dotnet/aspnet-blazor-eng Can one of you double check my treatment of `NavigationException` in this PR? I think I've covered every place we gracefully handle these exceptions, but I want to double check. I saw that `Renderer.HandleExceptionViaErrorBoundary` special cases `NavigationException`, but it doesn't look like any of the derived renderers do anything special with it in `HandleException`. It appears to me to just be a way to short circuit and prevent error boundaries from handling them, so I left that method untouched.

https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/b5a97c44d34062a3b8eba877c71732343c1f51a9/src/Components/Components/src/RenderTree/Renderer.cs#L1083-L1090
